


OPENSSH:
````````
Secure Shell, it is used to access remote machine's on CLI.

Package: openssh-server
Port: 22
Service: ssh


ON SERVER:
``````````

1) Install SSH:
```````````````
$ sudo apt install openssh-server -y

2) Start SSH Service:
``````````````````````
$ sudo systemctl <action> <service_name>

systemctl: command is used to handle Linux services, like start, stop, status, restart etc...

$ sudo systemctl start ssh
$ sudo systemctl status ssh
	OR
$ sudo systemctl is-active ssh

To enable this service in booting seq:

$ sudo systemctl enable ssh

To disabled this service in booting seq:

$ sudo systemctl disable ssh

3) To verify the port 22 is listening:
```````````````````````````````````````
$ sudo lsof -i:22


ON CLIENT:
``````````

1) Install Client side package:

$ sudo apt install openssh-client -y

2) Access remote machine via ssh:
`````````````````````````````````

$ ssh x.x.x.x  [By default it will try to access 'root' on remote machine]

$ ssh username@x.x.x.x
